A||||3||FieroSTETZ||help with CTS||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||02-07-2000||10:42 PM||||okay, according to my wiring diagrams (both haynes and chilton's) there should be a yellow and a black wire to the coolant temperature sensor on my 1986 SE 2M6- i can find no sensor with a yellow and black.. i pulled what the manuals said is the coolant sensor, but its a single green wire, and the new sensor i bought at carquest has 2 terminals... someone PLEASE send a pic or illustration showing the EXACT location of the coolant sensor... some should remember how long i have been asking for this. i have gotten many promises of pics, but not one has been given. can someone please help me?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||02-08-2000||02:17 AM||||There are 2 coolant temp switches. The first has a green and white wire and controls the engine blower motor and radiator fan. The second one has two wires one is tan the other is dark green this controls the temp light and the temp gauge. The one with one wire is the "Coolant Temp SWITCH" The one with two wires is the "Coolant temp SENSOR".<BR>The TEMP SWITCH is located left hand side looks like on the head by the ignition coil. The TEMP SENSOR is on the right hand side of block looks to be mounted either to the block or the bottom edge of the intake manifold. It's hard to tell from the illistration. Right now I cannot look at my car. If you still have problems let me know via E-mail and I'll scan the pages in. They are from the 1986 Pontiac Fiero Factory Service Manual. Hope this helps.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-08-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-08-2000||07:39 AM||||I just changed the Coolant Temperature Sensor (AutoZone $7.99) on my 85SE v-6.  It is located beneath the EGR solenoid and is threaded into a bushing in the intake manifold directly below the thermostat housing.  It is situated horizontally and is facing the passenger side of the car.  I believe the one you are looking at that has the one breen/white wire is next to the thermostat housing (slightly forward) and is facing up.<P>To get to mine I removed the egr solenoid (good time to check the vacuum hoses) and its mounting bracket.  Two bolts will get you there.  You will then be able to see the Temp sensor and get a deep socket on it.  It's a fairly easy job.  Don't forget to note which vacuum hose goes where since the two rear facing hoses look similar.  On mine the large vacuum hose that connects to the front of the solenoid and goes to the steel tube along the  firewall below the rear glass was getting pretty soft where it connects to the solenoid.  I believe it is 7/16" ID tubing (could be wrong here).<P>Don't forget to drain the coolant a bit or else it will come gushing out when you remove the sensor.  I jacked up the car on the right side and drained about 1/2 gallon from the coolant pipe under the passenger rocker panel.  It has a hex plug just in front of the rear tire well.  Be careful though because I've heard that these can break if they are seized up.  Mine came out without any troubles.<P>The other temp sensor with the tan and green wires is located in the head just above #5 spark plug (driver's side rear bank) next to the coil.  As Oreif mentioned this one controls the temp light and gauge.  It is facing kind of left rear sort of horizontally (if I recall correctly).<P>My Coolant Temperature Sensor started acting up and caused the engine to buck harshly and cut out.  It would do this after the engine reached operating temp and eventually set a code 15 (coolant temp sensor) and a code 35 (Idle air control valve).  The new Coolant temp sensor corrected the problem.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||02-08-2000||07:40 AM||||I just changed the Coolant Temperature Sensor (AutoZone $7.99) on my 85SE v-6.  It is located beneath the EGR solenoid and is threaded into a bushing in the intake manifold directly below the thermostat housing.  It is situated horizontally and is facing the passenger side of the car.  I believe the one you are looking at that has the one breen/white wire is next to the thermostat housing (slightly forward) and is facing up.<P>To get to mine I removed the egr solenoid (good time to check the vacuum hoses) and its mounting bracket.  Two bolts will get you there.  You will then be able to see the Temp sensor and get a deep socket on it.  It's a fairly easy job.  Don't forget to note which vacuum hose goes where since the two rear facing hoses look similar.  On mine the large vacuum hose that connects to the front of the solenoid and goes to the steel tube along the  firewall below the rear glass was getting pretty soft where it connects to the solenoid.  I believe it is 7/16" ID tubing (could be wrong here).<P>Don't forget to drain the coolant a bit or else it will come gushing out when you remove the sensor.  I jacked up the car on the right side and drained about 1/2 gallon from the coolant pipe under the passenger rocker panel.  It has a hex plug just in front of the rear tire well.  Be careful though because I've heard that these can break if they are seized up.  Mine came out without any troubles.<P>The other temp sensor with the tan and green wires is located in the head just above #5 spark plug (driver's side rear bank) next to the coil.  As Oreif mentioned this one controls the temp light and gauge.  It is facing kind of left rear sort of horizontally (if I recall correctly).<P>My Coolant Temperature Sensor started acting up and caused the engine to buck harshly and cut out.  It would do this after the engine reached operating temp and eventually set a code 15 (coolant temp sensor) and a code 35 (Idle air control valve).  The new Coolant temp sensor corrected the problem.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
